193
00:20:27,520 --> 00:20:36,640
And if no event has happened in that second timer, all of the events that have happened first get sent back to the get sent back to Apple.

194
00:20:37,520 --> 00:20:38,880
No other events get sent further.

195
00:20:39,520 --> 00:20:43,520
And I'm probably definitely grossly simplifying with some of this.

196
00:20:44,400 --> 00:20:48,640
And I have a YouTube video that goes into all of this into the V2.

197
00:20:48,800 --> 00:20:49,920
People can check that out.

198
00:20:50,800 --> 00:21:05,120
But my point being the fact that nearly all the trials happen within the first 24 hours make it relatively easy.ier for subscription apps to have the signal being captured by SDR networks.

199
00:21:05,440 --> 00:21:09,120
That's another reason why subscription apps do quite well.

200
00:21:10,240 --> 00:21:17,200
And obviously, you know, the more signal you give to the algorithm, the better the algorithm performs, right?

201
00:21:17,760 --> 00:21:24,160
You know, in the pre-ATT world, if you give more purchases, the better the algorithm performs.

202
00:21:24,320 --> 00:21:28,240
Obviously, the algorithm would take purchases from you and everybody else in the world and it would just do better.

203
00:21:28,720 --> 00:21:32,800
Now, obviously, it's just taking your trials and doing much, much better.

204
00:21:33,760 --> 00:21:41,120
And another trend we've seen is that web-based flows work a lot better for subscription apps than for games.

205
00:21:42,240 --> 00:21:53,920
Again, there are challenges in execution, but certainly one of the things they've seen that a lot of the relatively most successful apps are doing in the post-ATT world is adopt web-based flows.

206
00:21:55,200 --> 00:21:55,360
Right?

207
00:21:56,560 --> 00:22:06,480
So I would say those are some of the factors that I think contribute to subscription apps being better off than games in the post-ATT world.

208
00:22:07,120 --> 00:22:09,120
Again, not to grandstand, not to declare this a.

209
00:22:09,680 --> 00:22:12,400
victory yet, but I think they're much, much better off.

210
00:22:12,720 --> 00:22:16,080
Yeah, there's still also just the dynamic with consumable games.

211
00:22:16,320 --> 00:22:40,480
Like, I don't know what retention curves really look like and stuff like this, but with subscriptions, your acquisitions you're making today only affect, you know, a chunk of your revenue in the very short term versus, and you have this recurring user base versus consumables if your new users dry up really fast, like you're suddenly, you've suddenly lost a lot of, yeah, a lot of your business model doesn't work as well, right?

212
00:22:40,720 --> 00:22:41,200
So, yeah.

213
00:22:41,840 --> 00:22:43,200
But wow, that's incredible that.

214
00:22:43,920 --> 00:22:51,440
So on those CPIs for like social casinos or whatever, which I imagine is just a high spend category, highly competitive space.

215
00:22:51,760 --> 00:22:56,800
So if they don't have like value attribution, what's actually driving the CPI so high?

216
00:22:57,200 --> 00:23:00,640
Like how do they know like what users to spend that much money on?

217
00:23:00,880 --> 00:23:03,600
Is it just, is it just, I guess, click base?

218
00:23:03,920 --> 00:23:19,840
Like they still like they can they can proxy and know like people who click on those are part of that high value group or or what what keeps the what keeps the targeting good enough so that, you know, because you can imagine if everything was perfectly anonymous, all CPIs would clicks would be the same right across all apps.

219
00:23:20,160 --> 00:23:21,120
Yeah, yeah, yeah.

220
00:23:22,160 --> 00:23:28,960
At this point, I don't I wouldn't say we have a perfect answer for apps with high CPA.

221
00:23:29,760 --> 00:24:00,880
I think the best we have right now is treat Facebook reported metrics or platform reported metrics as directional, which means your CPA today would not be comparable to your pre ATT CPA, but because it's going to be very, very high just because of the privacy threshold that I just described that if you're getting maybe $500 CPA on this and $700 on that., then you just infer campaign A is better than campaign B, but you're not inferring that that's your actual cost of acquisition.

222
00:24:01,360 --> 00:24:04,160
So you're taking the CPA as a relative measure.

223
00:24:04,640 --> 00:24:08,880
And I think that's true for the casual game as it is for subscription apps.

224
00:24:09,200 --> 00:24:18,240
You're taking the CPA as a relative measure between campaign A and campaign B and not so much as an absolute measure of unit economics.

225
00:24:18,720 --> 00:24:24,800
I think that's a great point to transition to what's actually working right now.

226
00:24:25,200 --> 00:24:27,760
So we're talking about some of the impacts, but.

227
00:24:29,280 --> 00:24:44,480
I mean, what you're kind of hinting at is something that you've mentioned before is that the best source of truth now is not these highly specific return on ad spend calculation, but actually using blended metrics.

228
00:24:44,640 --> 00:24:59,760
So tell me a little bit about how how you approach thinking about blended metrics as a source of truth versus, you know, the past, you know, five or six years where it's way more focused on, um, very detailed return on ad spend.

229
00:25:00,080 --> 00:25:05,200
And again, to our earlier point, even if that return on ad spend calculation wasn't actually as accurate as it seemed.

230
00:25:06,640 --> 00:25:09,120
You were at least able to calculate it more accurately.

231
00:25:09,280 --> 00:25:11,280
Now it's like kind of everything's out the window.

232
00:25:11,840 --> 00:25:17,440
So how are you approaching blended spend or blended metrics to measure these things?

233
00:25:18,560 --> 00:25:26,080
Yeah, I will add the caveat that the blended metrics isn't like modern or new, right?

234
00:25:26,320 --> 00:25:30,800
It's what old school offline advertising worked with, right?

235
00:25:30,960 --> 00:25:33,520
They were like, oh, this is how much I spent, this is how much I made.

236
00:25:33,760 --> 00:25:36,560
That's how they measured everything before the internet, right?

237
00:25:36,960 --> 00:25:38,240
And even with the internet.

238
00:25:38,640 --> 00:25:51,040
Like a couple of companies we worked with, even pre ATT worked with blended metrics because they're like, look, we know that a portion of our paid installs drive organic.

239
00:25:51,520 --> 00:25:54,880
We have a very, very clear correlation between our paid and organic.

240
00:25:55,520 --> 00:26:02,320
So we would be, we would be leaving money on the table if all we took into account were paid users and not organic.

241
00:26:02,880 --> 00:26:05,680
So people we work with have definitely done that.

242
00:26:05,840 --> 00:26:09,520
And there are companies that have done it just to pursue top line growth.

243
00:26:10,080 --> 00:26:12,240
They're like, look, we need to grow as aggressively as possible.

244
00:26:13,120 --> 00:26:21,920
And the way to do that is to take Glenn's metrics to justify the growth rather than to shackle ourselves by taking just paid ROAS.

245
00:26:22,400 --> 00:26:26,160
But that said, to your question, how tactically, how do we sort of look at this?

246
00:26:26,320 --> 00:26:48,480
It just basically means, instead of saying, this campaign gave us a D7 return on ad spend of 20% or D7 cost per trial of $30, you're basically saying your overall marketing spend gave you...

247
00:26:48,480 --> 00:26:57,600
give you a $20 cost per trial across state and organic and social and everything that you're spending on.

248
00:26:58,000 --> 00:26:58,160
Right?

249
00:26:58,320 --> 00:27:08,640
Obviously that means a lot of people tend to be uncomfortable with that because they're like, oh, you know, if I hadn't spent on marketing, I would still have gotten 200 trials a day.

250
00:27:09,920 --> 00:27:12,160
And I'm giving credit to marketing for that.

251
00:27:12,480 --> 00:27:27,600
And, you know, I don't have a direct answer to that, but I think the answer really is, would you want to be, would you want a model that's helpful in helping you grow or would you want a model that's accurate but is it helping you grow?

252
00:27:28,160 --> 00:27:30,240
And I'm not going to claim I have an answer to that one.

253
00:27:31,280 --> 00:27:38,240
But yeah, so you're basically looking at your total number of trials and your total spend.

254
00:27:40,080 --> 00:28:01,360
And obviously this calculation becomes hearier if you have multiple channels, if you're running Google, Facebook, Snap, and multiple ad networks, then you're like, oh, you know, one of the ad networks probably performed badly, but my total blended not changed all that much because my other channel was like 10 percent of the end stock.

255
00:28:01,520 --> 00:28:16,160
So there are challenges, especially at large levels of spend, but it is a very solid source of truth, especially for smaller advertisers who may be on a handful of channels because comes out of your ITS.

256
00:28:16,560 --> 00:28:17,680
It's what you see in the bank.

257
00:28:18,240 --> 00:28:25,760
And you also mentioned that sending people, you mentioned web flows are working really well.

258
00:28:26,000 --> 00:28:53,120
And I assume what you meant by that is sending people from an ad into onto the web instead of onto the app store, which is really fascinating to me on multiple levels because the app stores have always been this black box where you put a certain number of clicks into it and then you see the end result but you don't see any of the steps in between.

259
00:28:53,280 --> 00:28:57,760
I mean, you have some basic metrics with app store analytics and stuff.

260
00:28:58,880 --> 00:29:07,040
But with the web, I would imagine that that gives you a more direct trackable link from somebody who.

261
00:29:08,960 --> 00:29:13,120
season ad to then actually kind of what they're doing on your website.

262
00:29:13,440 --> 00:29:24,480
So but then ultimately I've talked to a lot of developers who talk about how on the web their conversions are actually quite a bit lower than in the app because Apple's made it so easy to use in that purchase.

263
00:29:25,280 --> 00:29:44,480
So but it seems like maybe there is some of a balance there is that maybe you lose fewer people from having to jump through those hoops of installing the app before they even get to the onboarding before they can be shown you know the value proposition and then being you know shown a subscription page or whatever it is.

264
00:29:44,960 --> 00:29:51,520
What have you seen working in regard to web flows and then and specifically for subscription apps?

265
00:29:52,160 --> 00:29:52,960
Yeah, yeah, yeah.

266
00:29:53,120 --> 00:30:05,680
Like I said, I've certainly seen a lot of success for subscription apps that have adopted web flows and coupless apps I've worked with they've seen month on month growth through ATT.

267
00:30:07,120 --> 00:30:13,600
Again, not saying that's a typical result, but it definitely does happen.

268
00:30:14,400 --> 00:30:19,600
I think a couple of elements, you know, I think it's what's most important is to make sure that the flow is right.

269
00:30:19,840 --> 00:30:22,080
And there's a couple of possible flows.

270
00:30:23,680 --> 00:30:27,680
I think it's important to think through which one is right and really figure out what's right.

271
00:30:28,000 --> 00:30:30,880
And I think one flow could be show an ad.

272
00:30:31,600 --> 00:30:39,600
A user goes to a landing page, which is basically like a B2B or a B2C landing page on the web, what you would get for a web product.

273
00:30:40,720 --> 00:30:45,440
And you have a link that accepts the call to action on the landing pages.

274
00:30:45,920 --> 00:30:46,720
Go to the app store.

275
00:30:47,840 --> 00:30:52,400
So a lot of the experience, purchase and trial experience happens on the app store.

276
00:30:53,360 --> 00:30:59,520
The web page actually does the job of selling the user on the product.

277
00:31:00,400 --> 00:31:14,880
And it's my hypothesis that this actually works well because the web page can do a much, much better job of selling than the app store can while still making it clear that this is an app and while the actual conversion happens within the app itself.

278
00:31:16,560 --> 00:31:36,080
A somewhat similar, a different flow that's very comparable would be take a user to an ad, take a user from an ad to a landing page where a user has to input their phone number number, which again makes it clear to the user that this is an app, this is a mobile experience.

279
00:31:36,480 --> 00:31:39,760
The user gets a text message and the user signs up for it.

280
00:31:40,960 --> 00:31:45,360
And when they click on the text message, they get to go to the app store and download the app.

281
00:31:47,520 --> 00:31:58,640
Again, another model could be a user clicks on an ad, goes to an article or a content page, which is what you would see if you had a tabula or an outnought brain ad.

282
00:31:58,960 --> 00:32:02,800
Again, the article or a content page leads to an app store.

283
00:32:04,160 --> 00:32:11,200
And I think the last and I think the more complex flow is just to have onboard them on the web.

284
00:32:12,160 --> 00:32:16,640
Basically, take them to a web page and they onboard on the web.

285
00:32:17,760 --> 00:32:23,200
And they hopefully also actually make the purchase on the web, in which case you save 30%.

286
00:32:23,600 --> 00:32:27,120
This is, to be honest, the hardest and the most resource intensive.

287
00:32:27,520 --> 00:32:45,760
And really, it's my recommendation that you pursue that path after you have pursued one of the other paths that I recommended because you don't want to invest a ton of engineering and development time in building a web onboarding flow if you don't even know that the web flow is going to work for you.

288
00:32:46,640 --> 00:32:51,920
So I would recommend just testing the web landing pages first and then do the web onboarding flow.

289
00:32:52,640 --> 00:32:57,600
But yeah, I think those are some of the most important models that we see work.

290
00:32:58,320 --> 00:33:12,720
Something else I think that's also very, very critical is I think a lot of people when they look at a lot of advertisers I know that have started on the web for the first time were like, oh, let's put together this nice landing page that looks like our homepage.

291
00:33:13,760 --> 00:33:15,600
on our website and just put it out there.

292
00:33:17,280 --> 00:33:27,440
It doesn't work very well unless you've been very intentional about what value propositions to touch on the actual layout of your landing page.

293
00:33:27,600 --> 00:33:41,200
And we have a structure that we use now, which is like your most important pain point and value proposition goes in the header, then there's social proof, then your most important emotional benefits, then the features.

294
00:33:41,600 --> 00:33:50,800
I think the most successful advertisers we work with are very, very intentional about what that web page is looking like and they also test rigorously.

295
00:33:51,760 --> 00:33:56,400
I think that all of those elements are very, very critical to making the web flow work.

296
00:33:56,880 --> 00:33:58,080
Yeah, that's really smart.

297
00:33:58,240 --> 00:34:12,080
And I hadn't thought of it quite that way about how, you know, and I was talking with the app store being the black box, is you just sending someone, hoping they look at the screenshots, hoping the icon resonates with them, hoping the title and subtitle are meaningful.

298
00:34:13,040 --> 00:34:29,600
But when you send them to the web, it's not just about, you know, pushing them right to subscribe on the web, but it's actually just having a better opportunity to communicate the value prop so that by the time they get to the app store they are they have a much higher incentive.

299
00:34:29,840 --> 00:34:35,440
They have a higher they're just more likely to actually take action by the time they do get to the app store.

300
00:34:35,680 --> 00:34:36,640
So that makes a lot of sense.

301
00:34:36,960 --> 00:34:40,960
It tells you a lot about the quality of like the app store as a sales pitch, right?

302
00:34:41,760 --> 00:34:41,840
Yeah.

303
00:34:42,080 --> 00:34:45,120
I guess when you're like looking at a you know, you're trying to differentiate, right?

304
00:34:45,520 --> 00:34:49,200
And there's only so much you can communicate in a block of text and then a bunch of screenshots, right?

305
00:34:49,520 --> 00:34:54,400
You've seen so much data shoved into the screenshots on apps on apps, right?

306
00:34:54,560 --> 00:34:55,680
They're not screenshots, right?

307
00:34:55,920 --> 00:34:57,120
They're like a slide deck.

308
00:34:57,280 --> 00:34:57,360
Yeah.

309
00:34:57,840 --> 00:34:58,480
Little billboards.

310
00:34:59,120 --> 00:34:59,200
Yeah.

311
00:34:59,440 --> 00:34:59,520
Yeah.

312
00:34:59,840 --> 00:34:59,840
Yeah.

313
00:35:00,240 --> 00:35:00,320
Yeah.

314
00:35:00,720 --> 00:35:12,160
I also think another reason why the App Store worked so well pre ATT was Facebook would just show ads to users who installed other subscription apps.

315
00:35:12,800 --> 00:35:17,600
So if you send them directly to the app, they're almost prequalified, which is great.

316
00:35:17,920 --> 00:35:18,640
He's saying more.

317
00:35:19,840 --> 00:35:24,240
So I think the webflows level the field a lot.

318
00:35:24,960 --> 00:35:25,040
Yeah.

319
00:35:25,840 --> 00:35:31,760
It's a tough skill set for a lot of developers because they don't often have web experience internally.

320
00:35:32,160 --> 00:35:46,240
And I think, I think I hear so much like people get so obsessed about the thirty percent and they want to jump straight to that last one you mentioned about building a whole online purchasing thing, which like Stripe is pretty easy to use.

321
00:35:47,760 --> 00:35:52,960
It's not that much more work than building a landing page, but you have to remember, okay, management.

322
00:35:53,120 --> 00:35:55,600
So now you have to have a link where someone can go and cancel that thing.

323
00:35:55,760 --> 00:35:57,280
Now you also have to worry about taxes.

324
00:35:57,520 --> 00:36:00,640
Stripe doesn't like collecting tax information for you already.

325
00:36:01,200 --> 00:36:04,560
You have to then synchronize that with your back end.

326
00:36:04,880 --> 00:36:09,520
And if you're using Revit account, you synchronize with us or whatever, but you've got to manage all that too.

327
00:36:10,000 --> 00:36:13,200
It adds a lot of complexity for thirty percent, right?

328
00:36:13,440 --> 00:36:17,600
And when you're just trying to, you know, all of these things can find incremental growth.

329
00:36:18,000 --> 00:36:25,920
But like as you're saying, it's important to put them in the right order or you can end up wasting a lot of energy and money.

330
00:36:26,320 --> 00:36:26,480
Yeah.

331
00:36:26,800 --> 00:36:26,880
Yeah.

332
00:36:27,760 --> 00:36:31,360
Well, I did want to move on to the future.

333
00:36:32,000 --> 00:36:44,080
So we've kind of gotten through the first couple of months of this rough patch and are into this new era of mobile advertising.

334
00:36:44,640 --> 00:36:50,960
Are there anything that you're seeing that are especially promising that you think are the future?

335
00:36:51,520 --> 00:37:09,040
Like is the future everything we've been discussing so far of just 50% of your advertising works and 50% doesn't and you're never going to know which or are there some technologies coming online or some approaches that are just going to take time to kind of work out?

336
00:37:10,400 --> 00:37:12,400
Yeah, I think there's going to be some changes.

337
00:37:12,800 --> 00:37:17,520
I don't know if these are going to be but shattering in terms of changing.

338
00:37:18,720 --> 00:37:20,800
what's natural normal with ATT.

339
00:37:21,600 --> 00:37:27,120
I think the most promising though, I would say, is IOS 15 custom product pages.

340
00:37:28,560 --> 00:37:42,720
Basically, you know, it solves the problem that JKV alluded to, which is you have one small slide deck and everybody sees the same thing and Astra does a terrible job of telling a user on what the product is.

341
00:37:43,280 --> 00:37:52,960
Basically, with the custom product pages, you can have up to 35 versions of your app store, which means you're like, you know, very.

342
00:37:53,280 --> 00:38:14,880
Wellness app, let's just say you're like a meditation app that has meditations for sleep, for anxiety and how to meditate, you can have a separate landing page, so to speak, on the App Store for sleep, anxiety, meditation, right, and you can send, you get a unique URL for each of these.

343
00:38:15,440 --> 00:38:23,120
So presumably you can have an ad for sleep going to an App Store for sleep, an ad for anxiety going to an App Store for anxiety.

344
00:38:24,000 --> 00:38:25,040
I think that can help.

345
00:38:25,440 --> 00:38:27,680
I just don't think it's going to help too much on the measurement front.

346
00:38:28,960 --> 00:38:33,680
Obviously, the actual execution is still unclear, the announcement's out.

347
00:38:33,840 --> 00:38:40,400
So I think definitely one of the bigger changes, I would say, that's coming with IS 15.

348
00:38:41,600 --> 00:38:54,720
The other one would just be that advertisers are going to be receiving postbacks, which is huge, at least in ensuring accuracy of the advertising data so far.

349
00:38:55,200 --> 00:39:03,040
What's completely bonkers right now is networks like Facebook, Snap, everybody gets your postback from Escape.

350
00:39:03,040 --> 00:39:44,400
back from SCAD network but you as an advertiser you as an advertiser do not which means you basically take the word for it I do know for a fact that TikTok has actually changed values I don't want to call it malicious because the conversion value was null and they changed it to zero the problem is that null and zero have very very different meanings zero means install and null means an install happen and there's just no value so I know that they did that change I don't know if they're continuing to do it so but my point is And Google explicitly says we're going to use modeled conversions, which basically means take our word for it.

351
00:39:45,280 --> 00:39:47,280
Facebook says campaign data is accurate.

352
00:39:47,440 --> 00:39:50,560
Everything underneath is modeled, which means take our word for it.

353
00:39:53,360 --> 00:39:59,280
With all of this is because the postpack goes to the networks but not the advertiser.

354
00:40:00,080 --> 00:40:17,680
With IOS 15, if the postpack goes to the advertiser, you can at the very least verify if they're telling the truth, which is bonkers considering, I think, David, you were mentioning, until all this time, you just have to take the whole platform's word for it, even pre ATT.

355
00:40:19,440 --> 00:40:26,640
So I think that's going to be a big, big change, even though a lot of that's going to happen under the hood.

356
00:40:26,800 --> 00:40:35,600
And I say advertisers for the vast majority of advertisers, the MMP is going to do their receiving of the postpacks, but I think that's going to be a big deal.

357
00:40:36,640 --> 00:40:40,960
I think those are the big changes, the custom product pages and the postpacks to advertisers.

358
00:40:41,520 --> 00:40:47,600
But really, in terms of the future, in many ways, I do think it's going to be back to 2013 or 2014.

359
00:40:48,560 --> 00:40:57,600
I think I talked about how we bought a number of installs and we hoped that a number, certain percentages, well, we knew that a certain percentage of them would convert to purchases.

360
00:40:57,760 --> 00:40:59,040
That was before subscription.

361
00:40:59,120 --> 00:41:00,400
So there were a lot of files.

362
00:41:00,880 --> 00:41:02,880
But I think it's going to be a very similar world.

363
00:41:03,120 --> 00:41:09,280
We're going to be, you're going to have to be more comfortable making decisions based off incomplete data.

364
00:41:10,640 --> 00:41:12,320
But I do see that being the future.

365
00:41:12,960 --> 00:41:20,240
One of the things I've been hearing a lot about since Apple announced ATT last year is incrementality testing.

366
00:41:20,560 --> 00:41:45,840
So systematically turning on and off, you know, so if you're advertising, I mean, obviously this would be a tool for larger apps, but if you're advertising across Facebook, Google, Snap, TikTok, and, you know, other mobile DSPs, you know, systematically moving spend around and then measuring the difference or even turning spend off in certain channels and increasing spend in other channels.

367
00:41:47,600 --> 00:41:49,520
Have you seen that work?

368
00:41:50,640 --> 00:41:57,760
And are you excited about the potential of having tools in this space or do you think incrementality?

369
00:41:58,320 --> 00:41:59,600
testing is a bit overhyped?

370
00:42:00,000 --> 00:42:11,600
So I think with any recommendation like incrementality, I think it's the one caveat that a lot of people miss is that it's useful for a very, very tiny fraction of advertisers.

